# Direct dependencies, with floors. requirements.txt is the lock generated from
# this file and is what the deploy installs; never edit it by hand. Re-lock with:
#
#   cd backend && uv pip compile requirements.in --python-version 3.12 \
#     --python-platform x86_64-manylinux_2_28 \
#     --exclude-newer 2026-08-03T09:14:00Z \
#     --exclude-newer-package pyjwt=2026-10-21T00:00:00Z \
#     --exclude-newer-package msal=2026-10-21T00:00:00Z \
#     --generate-hashes -o requirements.txt
#
# --python-platform resolves for Flex Consumption's Linux x86-64 workers rather
# than for the machine running the command. --exclude-newer is the instant the
# last v1 deploy finished, when its build resolved these same floors unpinned,
# so the lock reproduces as closely as can now be known what the live app has
# run since, and the command gives the same answer on any later day.
# --generate-hashes puts pip in hash-checking mode: a file that differs from the
# one locked fails the install instead of shipping.
#
# Delete requirements.txt before running the command. uv treats the pins in an
# existing output file as preferences and keeps any that still satisfy the
# constraints, so compiling over the old lock gives an answer that depends on
# the old lock rather than on this command: the Phase 1 re-lock kept msal at
# 1.37.0 that way, where the command alone resolves 1.39.0.
#
# Moving that date, or adding a package, is a deliberate re-lock and never a
# side effect. Phase 1 made the first one (2026-10-21): pyjwt 2.15.1 and msal
# come in through the two per-package --exclude-newer-package overrides above,
# which move only those two packages past the v1 instant. Everything they pull
# in transitively (cryptography, requests) still resolves at 2026-08-03, so the
# rest of the lock is the one the live app already runs.
#
# pytest is not listed because it never ships; install it into the venv
# separately.

# Azure Functions Python v2 programming model.
# >=1.24.0 is the floor for the GA MCP extension (planned for M7).
azure-functions>=1.24.0

# Data + identity. Cosmos data-plane auth is RBAC-only (disableLocalAuth), so
# DefaultAzureCredential is the only way in — there is no key to fall back to.
azure-cosmos>=4.9.0
azure-identity>=1.19.0

# Foundry / Azure OpenAI.
openai>=1.60.0

# Fetching. http2 extra pulls in `h2`, which APNs requires — a silent HTTP/1.1
# fallback is rejected outright by Apple.
httpx[http2]>=0.28.0
feedparser>=6.0.11

# Article extraction. Pin >=1.8.0: earlier releases were GPLv3+, 2.x is Apache-2.0.
trafilatura>=2.1.0

# Config + vector math.
PyYAML>=6.0.2
numpy>=2.1.0

# Entra External ID access-token validation (core/auth.py), and APNs JWT
# signing (ES256) later. Pinned exactly rather than floored, because auth.py
# depends on what 2.15 changed in PyJWKClient: 2.13 (the previous lock) refetches
# the key set for every unknown `kid`, so a stream of tokens with made-up kids
# becomes a stream of JWKS fetches, and it follows redirects. 2.15 allows one
# forced refetch per 30-second cooldown, serialises lookups behind a lock and
# refuses redirects. A token validator is the one dependency whose drift should
# always be a reviewed change. The crypto extra brings in `cryptography`, without
# which RS256 verification is unavailable.
pyjwt[crypto]==2.15.1

# Owner tooling: scripts/get_token.py and scripts/mynews_admin.py sign in with
# the mynews-cli app registration (public client, auth code + PKCE), and graph-
# mode identity removal (Later) uses client credentials. azure-identity already
# depends on it; listing it states that this code imports it directly, so the
# floor no longer depends on what azure-identity happens to require.
msal>=1.31.0
